{"step_index":155,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-13T06:14:57Z","input_tokens":1258,"cache_read_tokens":98271,"output_tokens":671,"content":"Everything is committed. Here's what was built:\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Mobile App Structure — Complete\n\n### Navigation Architecture\n\n```\nRoot _layout.tsx  (ThemeProvider + QueryClientProvider + useProtectedRoute)\n│\n├── (auth)/              ← Stack, no header — only for unauthenticated users\n│   ├── login.tsx        ← Phone number form (react-hook-form + zod, E.164 validation)\n│   └── verify-otp.tsx  ← 6-digit box UI, resend timer, dev stub for signIn\n│\n└── (app)/               ← Protected layout: redirects to login if not authed\n    ├── profile.tsx      ← User info + role badge + sign-out\n    └── (tabs)/          ← Tabs navigator\n        ├── index.tsx    ← Role-aware dashboard (patient vs agent cards)\n        ├── referrals.tsx\n        └── wallet.tsx   ← \"Earnings\" for agents, \"Wallet\" for patients\n```\n\n### Auth Flow\n- `useAuthStore` (Zustand) hydrates from `expo-secure-store` on startup\n- Root layout's `useProtectedRoute` redirects once `isHydrated === true` — **no flash of wrong screen**\n- `(app)/_layout.tsx` has a **second-line `<Redirect />` guard** as belt-and-suspenders\n- Dev stub in `verify-otp.tsx` lets you test the full flow without a backend — it signs in with a fake token and navigates to the app immediately\n\n### Key files to wire up in Phase 3\n| File | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| [`login.tsx`](file:///Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main/src/app/(auth)/login.tsx) | Replace `// TODO` with `useRequestOtp()` hook |\n| [`verify-otp.tsx`](file:///Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main/src/app/(auth)/verify-otp.tsx) | Replace stub with `useVerifyOtp()` hook |\n| [`lib/api.ts`](file:///Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main/src/lib/api.ts) | Add token refresh in the 401 interceptor |\n| [`features/auth/hooks.ts`](file:///Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main/src/features/auth/hooks.ts) | Hooks are stubbed and typed — just uncomment the `api` calls |\n\n### To run the app now\n```bash\ncd /Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main\nnpm install       # install node_modules first\nnpx expo start    # tap into verify-otp to use the dev stub login\n```"}
{"step_index":156,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-13T06:16:10Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nHow the first complete Kezza feature should work\nInstead of building 20 screens independently, build one complete business journey from start to finish.      First end-to-end MVP journey\n1. Agent generates a referral link\n   \n   Each agent has a unique referral code.\n2. Patient registers\n   \n   The patient opens the link, verifies their mobile number and creates an account.\n3. Referral is linked\n   \n   The backend connects the patient's account with the referring agent and locks the attribution.\n4. Patient purchases a treatment\n   \n   Kezza verifies the actual collected payment.\n5. Patient receives rewards\n   \n   The backend credits eligible points to the patient's points ledger.\n6. Agent receives commission\n   \n   The backend determines the agent's commission tier and records the commission.\n7. Both see their results\n   \n   The patient sees treatment credit; the agent sees earnings and referral details.  This one workflow tests the entire foundation: authentication, referral attribution, sales, points, commissions, database and mobile API integration.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-11-13T11:46:10+05:30.\n\nThe user's current state is as follows:\nOther open documents:\n- /Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main/src/app/_layout.tsx (LANGUAGE_UNSPECIFIED)\n- /Users/ravi/Desktop/Kezza_mobile-main/backend/app/main.py (LANGUAGE_UNSPECIFIED)\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":157,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-13T06:16:10Z","input_tokens":1784,"cache_read_tokens":98797,"output_tokens":9891,"content":"This is the right architectural decision — one complete journey tests everything. Let me create a comprehensive blueprint then implement the full stack in parallel.","thinking":"The user wants to understand how the first complete Kezza feature should work - the end-to-end MVP journey. They want a comprehensive plan/document that maps out this entire flow. This seems like a planning/documentation request rather than a code-writing request, though they may want me to start implementing it.\n\nLet me think about what this involves:\n\n1. **Agent generates a referral link** - Agent has a unique code, generates a shareable link like `kezzamobile://ref/AGENT_CODE` or `https://kezza.app/ref/AGENT_CODE`\n\n2. **Patient registers** - Opens the link, app detects the referral code, patient verifies phone + creates account\nNext I'm working through the back half of the journey: the backend linking the patient to the agent without duplicates, the clinic payment getting verified, the points calculation for the patient, and the tiered commission for the agent. Then I'll check what both sides see on their mobile screens.\n\nI'm sketching out the backend models I'd need: OTP requests for phone verification, clinic payment records, patient points ledger and redemptions, commission tiers, and the agent commission ledger. I'm also listing the two OTP endpoints for requesting a code and verifying it while registering.\n\nNow I'm listing the endpoints for the agent referral link, creating referrals, recording payments, and reading the patient wallet and commission balances.\n\nI'm weighing whether to just draft a planning document or go straight into building the backend models, API endpoints, and mobile screens. The request reads as a plan, but the user seems to want implementation too, so I'm considering a parallel approach across backend and frontend.\n\nNext I'm planning out the back half of the implementation: a phone OTP flow, then auth endpoints, referral endpoints, payment and points endpoints, and commission endpoints, with the mobile side connecting login and building out the referral and earnings screens.\n\nStill defining the database models...\n\nStill sketching the commission ledger model...\n\nNow I'm listing the referral endpoints that the agent side will call, and the agent endpoints for their profile and referral link.\n\nNext I'm laying out the wallet endpoints for the patient's points balance and history, and the commission endpoints for the agent's balance and ledger. The payment endpoint is an internal one that records a clinic payment and triggers both points and commission. I'll draft the planning doc first, then build the Phase 2 models, the migration, the service layer, and the endpoints, and wire up the mobile screens.\n\nI'm settling on a sequential approach: a planning document first, then the backend models, migration, schemas, services, and endpoints, with the mobile screens wired to the real APIs after that.\n\nNext I'm defining the referral link setup: the agent's code goes into a deep link and a web link, with attribution saved on the referral record. After that I'm covering points at one per rand spent and the matching ledger entry, then the tiered commission, and finally what the patient and agent each see.\n\nI'm weighing how to get the JWT side in place: checking whether PyJWT or python-jose is already available in the venv, since requirements.txt only lists fastapi. For the OTP, I'll generate a six-digit code, hash it, store it in the database, and log it for now instead of sending an SMS.\n\nI'm moving on to the rest of the backend: the auth service with its OTP flow, the referral service, and the points and commission logic. After that I'll wire up the API endpoints and connect the mobile app. I still need to check which JWT library is installed in the venv.\n\nNext I'm setting up the rest of the backend files: schemas for referrals, wallet, and commissions, the auth, referral, points, and commission services, the endpoint modules for auth, agents, referrals, wallet, and payments, plus the router and a security module.\n\nI'm wrapping up the backend file list: the JWT and password hashing helpers, settings, the auth dependencies, and a new Alembic migration. Then I'm turning to the mobile screens, wiring login and OTP verification to the API hooks and pointing the home, referrals, and wallet tabs at real data. Before going further I want to settle on a single plan, so I'll write the planning doc first and then build.\n\nI'm settling on what the backend has to cover to make the journey work: JWT auth with the OTP flow, the referral link, patient registration tied to a referral code, payment recording with points, commission calculation, and the balance and ledger endpoints. I'll do the backend myself in one pass rather than splitting it across workers, so the code stays consistent, and then wire the mobile screens to it.\n\nI'm checking whether a JWT library is already in the venv, since requirements.txt only lists FastAPI and I can't install packages in this sandbox. I'll write the code against PyJWT and add it to the requirements, and I'm using hashlib's SHA-256 for hashing the OTP for now.\n\nI'm weighing whether to use a JWT library already in the venv, like python-jose, or to add PyJWT to the requirements. For the OTP itself I'm leaning toward a simple hash with the built-in hashlib module, and I'm also considering skipping hashing entirely for the MVP, though that would need to be clearly marked as insecure.\n\nI'm settling on `python-jose` for JWT and adding it to requirements, since the user can install it later, and using the built-in `secrets` module to generate a six-digit OTP that gets hashed with SHA-256 before storage.\n\nNext I'm sketching the request flow for the MVP