g a simpler HMAC-SHA256 token built from the standard library so I don't have to add a JWT dependency.\n\nWriting the token code as a self-contained HMAC function starts to look attractive, but a custom format is more hacky than it's worth, so I'll stick with a proper JWT library and add it to requirements.txt. I'm also checking whether `cryptography` is already in the backend venv...\n\nChecking the venv, I'll use `python-jose[cryptography]` for the JWT and note the dependency in requirements.txt. Then I'm moving into the build: a planning artifact and core modules first, then schemas and services, endpoints, the migration with main.py, and finally the mobile side.\n\nI'm checking whether the existing `base.py` and the `Base` class in `database.py` are duplicates that could conflict, since I remember both defining one.\n\nI'm checking whether `database.py` still carries its own `Base` class, since the original had one and my rewrite replaced that file. If it does, `base.py` is the only definition and the models will import from there. I'm also planning the code batches and will start with the config and security modules.\n\nI'm moving on from the models and schemas to services, endpoints, the migration, and the mobile screens, then committing. I've decided to hand the backend and mobile work to swarm workers so they run in parallel, and I'll coordinate and integrate the results myself. The shared API contract, starting with the auth endpoint, is what both sides need to agree on.\n\nI'm listing out the API endpoints the MVP needs, covering OTP request and verify, the referral link, wallet balance and ledger, and commission balance, and I'm planning to create the planning doc first, then hand the backend work to a single worker that has the full context.\n\nI'm going to write the planning artifact now, covering the architecture overview, the full journey sequence, the API contract, the points and commission data flow, and a phased implementation checklist. Then I'll 